**Trust And Authentication Model**

You noted an important condition: authentication and authorization are already handled by the portal backend.

That changes the design in a useful way. It means the new chat solution does not need to invent a second end-user identity model. Instead, it should preserve the portal’s trust boundary.

The practical trust model becomes:

```text
User authenticates with the portal
    |
    v
Portal decides the user is allowed to use the assistant
    |
    v
Frontend calls APIM/BFF as part of the portal experience
    |
    v
BFF authenticates to Foundry as an application identity
```

So there are two very different kinds of auth here:

**User auth**
- already handled by the portal side
- decides whether the person can use the chat feature

**Service-to-service auth**
- handled by managed identity and RBAC
- allows the Function to call Foundry securely

This separation is healthy. It avoids forcing Foundry to participate in the end-user login experience.

